export const lessons={direction:'Describe the body in anatomical position: upright, facing forward, palms forward. Right and left always belong to the person being described. A directional term compares two locations.',cavities:'Start with the two major compartments: dorsal and ventral. The diaphragm separates thoracic from abdominopelvic. Abdominal and pelvic cavities are continuous; there is no wall between them.',membranes:'Think “wall → parietal → thin fluid space → visceral → organ.” The space is enlarged in our diagram for visibility. A lung or heart is covered by a visceral layer; it does not float inside the potential serous space.',regions:'Read this anterior view from the patient’s perspective. Their right is on your left. The nine regions are surface map areas, not separate cavities. Organs can cross boundaries and vary with body position and fullness.',organs:'Identify the structure first, then connect it with its system. For this course, pancreas belongs to digestive and endocrine; ovaries and testes belong to reproductive and endocrine. These are course groupings, not an exhaustive list of every organ’s roles.'};
